</name></person-group><article-title>Suppression of an absolute defect in type IV pilus biogenesis by loss-of-function mutations in <italic>pilT</italic>, a twitching motility gene in <italic>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</italic></article-title><source>Proc Natl Acad Sci USA</source><year>1998</year><volume>95</volume><fpage>14973</fpage><lpage>14978</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9844000</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b62"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wolfgang</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Components and dynamics of fiber formation define a ubiquitous biogenesis pathway for bacterial pili</article-title><source>EMBO J</source><year>2000</year><volume>19</volume><fpage>6408</fpage><lpage>6418</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11101514</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b63"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Zhang</surname><given-names>QY</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Gene conversion in <italic>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</italic>: evidence for its role in pilus antigenic variation</article-title><source>Proc Natl Acad Sci USA</source><year>1992</year><volume>89</volume><fpage>5366</fpage><lpage>5370</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">1351681</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><sec sec-type="supplementary-material"><title>Supplementary material</title><p>The following supplementary material is available for this article:</p><supplementary-material content-type="local-data" id="SD1"><label>Fig. S1.</label><caption><p>Small amounts of oxonium ion for pilin glycan are detected in a <italic>pglF</italic>-null mutant. ESI mass spectra over a range of 180&#x02013;1800 <italic>m/z</italic>.</p><p>A. Strain N400 (wt, wild-type), glycan oxonium ion at <italic>m/z</italic> 433.2 is boxed.</p><p>B. Strain GGC (<italic>pgIC</italic>::<italic>kan</italic>).</p><p>C. Strain GGD (<italic>pglD</italic>::<italic>kan</italic>).</p><p>D. GGF (<italic>pglF</italic>::<italic>kan</italic>). The oxonium ion at <italic>m/z</italic> 433.2 is detected in the magnified conventional MS spectrum (boxed in right panel). A complete list of all oxonium ion species with <italic>m/z</italic> values is found in Table S1.</p></caption><media xlink:href="mmi0065-0607-s1.pdf" xlink:type="simple" id="N0x1c493f0N0x31f6190" position="anchor" mimetype="application" mime-subtype="pdf"/></supplementary-material><supplementary-material content-type="local-data" id="SD2"><label>Fig. S2.</label><caption><p><italic>N. gonorrhoeae pglE</italic> maps genetically downstream of <italic>pglA</italic>.</p><p>A. Deconvoluted molecular weight spectrum from intact PilE ESI-MS analyses showing the pilin species found in a <italic>pglE</italic><sub><italic>on</italic></sub>, <italic>pglA</italic> double mutant (strain KS143 <italic>pglE</italic><sub><italic>on</italic></sub>, <italic>pglA</italic>::<italic>kan</italic>). The asterisks mark peaks representing unknown contamination. A complete list of pilin modifications and masses of the corresponding PilE species is found in Table S1.</p><p>B. ESI mass spectrum over a range of 180&#x02013;1800 <italic>m/z</italic>. The oxonium ion for DATDH at <italic>m/z</italic> 229.1 (boxed) is detected. A complete list of all oxonium ion species with <italic>m/z</italic> values and corresponding molecular weight values of all PilE species are found in Table S1.</p></caption><media xlink:href="mmi0065-0607-s2.pdf" xlink:type="simple" id="N0x1c493f0N0x31f6250" position="anchor" mimetype="application" mime-subtype="pdf"/></supplementary-material><supplementary-material content-type="local-data" id="SD3"><label>Fig. S3.</label><caption><p><italic>N. gonorrhoeae pglI</italic> maps genetically downstream of <italic>pglA</italic></p><p>A. Deconvoluted molecular weight spectrum from intact PilE ESI-MS analysis showing the pilin species found in a <italic>pglI</italic>, <italic>pglA</italic> double mutant (strain KS140, <italic>pglA</italic>::<italic>erm</italic>, <italic>pglI</italic>::<italic>kan</italic>). A complete list of pilin modifications and masses of the corresponding PilE species is found in Table S1.</p><p>B. ESI mass spectrum over a range of 180&#x02013;1800 <italic>m/z</italic>. The oxonium ion for DATDH (boxed; <italic>m/z</italic> 229.1) is seen. A complete list of oxonium ions and corresponding <italic>m/z</italic> numbers is found in Table S1.</p></caption><media xlink:href="mmi0065-0607-s3.pdf" xlink:type="simple" id="N0x1c493f0N0x31f6310" position="anchor" mimetype="application" mime-subtype="pdf"/></supplementary-material><supplementary-material content-type="local-data" id="SD4"><label>Fig. S4.</label><caption><p><italic>C. jejuni pglA</italic> complements a <italic>pglA</italic>-null mutant in <italic>N. gonorrhoeae</italic>. Conventional ESI mass spectra over a range of 180&#x02013;1800 <italic>m/z</italic> from (A) strain KS149 (<italic>pglA::kan</italic>, <italic>iga::pglA</italic><sub><italic>Cj</italic></sub>); (B) strain KS150 (<italic>pglA::kan</italic>, <italic>iga::pglA</italic><sub><italic>Cj</italic></sub>, <italic>pglIfs</italic>); and (C) strain KS151 (<italic>pglA::kan</italic>, <italic>iga::pglA</italic><sub><italic>Cj</italic></sub>, <italic>pglE</italic><sub><italic>on</italic></sub>). The Gonococcal pilin glycosylation 623 predominant oxonium ion species are boxed in each panel: (A) <italic>m/z</italic> 474.2, (B) <italic>m/z</italic> 432.2 and (C) <italic>m/z</italic> 636.2. A complete list of all oxonium ion species with <italic>m/z</italic> values is found in Table S1.</p></caption><media xlink:href="mmi0065-0607-s4.pdf" xlink:type="simple" id="N0x1c493f0N0x31f63d0" position="anchor" mimetype="application" mime-subtype="pdf"/></supplementary-material><supplementary-material content-type="local-data" id="SD5"><label>Fig. S5.</label><caption><p>Evidence for double acteylation of the proximal Hex and HexNAc residues in wild-type and KS149 PilE disaccharide moieties.</p><p>A. CID MS/MS spectrum of the oxonium ion at <italic>m/z</italic> 475.2 (boxed; <italic>m/z</italic> 391.2 plus two times 42 Da, doubly acetylated HexDATDH) from the wild-type strain shows the fragmentation into an oxonium ion species at <italic>m/z</italic> 247.2 (boxed) corresponding to doubly acetylated Hex.</p><p>B. CID MS/MS spectrum of the oxonium ion at <italic>m/z</italic> 516.2 (boxed; <italic>m/z</italic> 432.2 plus two times 42 Da, doubly acetylated HexNAcDATDH) from strain KS149 (<italic>pglA::kan</italic>, <italic>iga::pglA</italic><sub><italic>Cj</italic></sub>) shows the fragmentation into an oxonium ion species at <italic>m/z</italic> 288.2 (boxed) corresponding to doubly acetylated HexNAc.</p></caption><media xlink:href="mmi0065-0607-s5.pdf" xlink:type="simple" id="N0x1c493f0N0x31f6490" position="anchor" mimetype="application" mime-subtype="pdf"/></supplementary-material><supplementary-material content-type="local-data" id="SD6"><label>Fig. S6.</label><caption><p>PglI shows a high degree of identity to characterized LPS <italic>O</italic>-acetyltransferases.</p><p>Upper part. Graphical overview of the domain structure of PglI. Colour codes: green, ProDom family PD274832 (residues 8&#x02013;70); yellow, Acyl_transf_3 domain PF01757 (residues 8&#x02013;343); red, transmembrane region (residues 350&#x02013;372); blue, low-complexity region (residues 573&#x02013;584).</p><p>Lower part. Alignment of PglI (NG0065) with the LPS <italic>O</italic>-acetylase Lot3 (<italic>N. meningitidis</italic>, NMA2202) and OafA (<italic>H. influenzae</italic>, HAEIN Y392). Colour codes: yellow background indicates identical residues, while blue background indicates similar residues.</p></caption><media xlink:href="mmi0065-0607-s6.pdf" xlink:type="simple" id="N0x1c493f0N0x31f6550" position="anchor" mimetype="application" mime-subtype="pdf"/></supplementary-material><supplementary-material content-type="local-data" id="SD7"><label>Table 1.</label><caption><p>A complete list of PilE modifications and corresponding masses or <italic>m/z</italic>.</p></caption><media xlink:href="mmi0065-0607-t1.pdf" xlink:type="simple" id="N0x1c493f0N0x31f6610" position="anchor" mimetype="application" mime-subtype="pdf"/></supplementary-material><p>This material is available as part of the online article from: <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://www.blackwell-synergy.com/doi/abs/10.1111/j.1365-2958.2007.05806.x">http://www.blackwell-synergy.com/doi/abs/10.1111/j.1365-2958.2007.05806.x</ext-link></p><p>(This link will take you to the article abstract).</p><p>Please note: Blackwell Publishing is not responsible for the content or functionality of any supplementary materials supplied by the authors. Any queries (other than issing material) should be directed to the corresponding author for the article.</p></sec></back></article>